@startuml 01-use-case
'============================================================================
'  Sunrise Dental Clinic - USE CASE DIAGRAM
'  CIS6003 Advanced Programming (WRIT1), Task A
'
'  Shows who uses the system and what they can do with it.
'
'  A NOTE ON <<include>> AND <<extend>>, WHICH ARE OFTEN USED THE WRONG WAY
'
'    <<include>>  the base use case ALWAYS performs the included one. It is
'                 shared behaviour that has been factored out. The arrow points
'                 FROM the base use case TO the one it includes.
'
'    <<extend>>   the extending use case happens only SOMETIMES, at a point in
'                 the base use case, when some condition holds. The arrow
'                 points FROM the extending use case TO the base one, which is
'                 the opposite direction, because the base does not know it
'                 exists.
'
'  So "Register appointment" always finds or creates the patient record, and
'  that is an include. It only suggests another time when the slot has just
'  gone, and that is an extend.
'============================================================================

'Layout is done by the Graphviz engine bundled with PlantUML.

left to right direction
skinparam packageStyle rectangle
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam usecase {
    BackgroundColor #F0FDFA
    BorderColor #0F766E
}
skinparam actor {
    BackgroundColor #CCFBF1
    BorderColor #0F766E
}
skinparam note {
    BackgroundColor #FFFBEB
    BorderColor #B45309
}

title Sunrise Dental Clinic - Appointment and Patient Management System

'--- the people and systems outside ----------------------------------------

actor "Receptionist" as Reception
actor "Administrator" as Admin
actor "Email service" as Mail <<external>>

' An administrator can do everything a receptionist can, and more. That is a
' generalisation, so every association drawn to Receptionist applies to the
' Administrator too and does not have to be drawn twice.
Admin --|> Reception

rectangle "Sunrise Dental Clinic System" {

    '--- signing in and out ------------------------------------------------
    usecase "Sign in\n(FR1)" as UC_SignIn
    usecase "Sign out\n(FR6)" as UC_SignOut
    usecase "Open help\n(FR5)" as UC_Help

    '--- appointments -------------------------------------------------------
    usecase "Register\nappointment\n(FR2)" as UC_Register
    usecase "Find and display\nappointment\n(FR3)" as UC_Display
    usecase "Cancel\nappointment\n(FR7)" as UC_Cancel
    usecase "Reschedule\nappointment\n(FR7)" as UC_Reschedule
    usecase "Mark visit\ncompleted" as UC_Complete
    usecase "Record\nno-show" as UC_NoShow

    '--- billing ------------------------------------------------------------
    usecase "Produce bill\n(FR4)" as UC_Bill
    usecase "Print bill\n(FR4)" as UC_PrintBill
    usecase "Record\npayment" as UC_Pay
    usecase "View unpaid\nbills" as UC_Unpaid

    '--- patients -----------------------------------------------------------
    usecase "Search\npatient" as UC_SearchPatient
    usecase "View treatment\nhistory\n(FR7)" as UC_History

    '--- reports ------------------------------------------------------------
    usecase "View day\nschedule" as UC_DaySchedule
    usecase "View revenue\nreport" as UC_Revenue
    usecase "View dentist\nworkload report" as UC_Workload

    '--- behaviour that is always part of something else (<<include>>) ------
    usecase "Find or create\npatient record" as UC_FindPatient
    usecase "Check dentist\navailability" as UC_CheckSlot
    usecase "Allocate reference\nnumber" as UC_RefNo
    usecase "Calculate\ncharge" as UC_Calculate

    '--- behaviour that happens only sometimes (<<extend>>) -----------------
    usecase "Suggest another\ntime" as UC_Suggest
    usecase "Apply agreed\ndiscount" as UC_Discount
    usecase "Apply loyalty\ndiscount" as UC_Loyalty
    usecase "Send appointment\nnotification" as UC_Notify
}

'--- who does what ----------------------------------------------------------

Reception --> UC_SignIn
Reception --> UC_SignOut
Reception --> UC_Help
Reception --> UC_Register
Reception --> UC_Display
Reception --> UC_Cancel
Reception --> UC_Reschedule
Reception --> UC_Complete
Reception --> UC_NoShow
Reception --> UC_Bill
Reception --> UC_PrintBill
Reception --> UC_Pay
Reception --> UC_Unpaid
Reception --> UC_SearchPatient
Reception --> UC_History
Reception --> UC_DaySchedule

' Only an administrator sees clinic-wide takings and staff performance.
Admin --> UC_Revenue
Admin --> UC_Workload

' The mail server is a supporting actor: the system talks to it, it never
' starts anything itself.
UC_Notify --> Mail

'--- always performed as part of the base use case --------------------------

UC_Register ..> UC_FindPatient : <<include>>
UC_Register ..> UC_CheckSlot   : <<include>>
UC_Register ..> UC_RefNo       : <<include>>
UC_Reschedule ..> UC_CheckSlot : <<include>>
UC_Bill ..> UC_Calculate       : <<include>>
UC_Bill ..> UC_RefNo           : <<include>>
UC_History ..> UC_SearchPatient : <<include>>

'--- performed only when a condition holds ----------------------------------

UC_Suggest  ..> UC_Register  : <<extend>>\n(the chosen slot\nis already taken)
UC_Notify   ..> UC_Register  : <<extend>>\n(the patient gave\nan email address)
UC_Discount ..> UC_Bill      : <<extend>>\n(the clinic agreed\na reduction)
UC_Loyalty  ..> UC_Calculate : <<extend>>\n(fifth or later\ncompleted visit)

'--- assumptions worth stating on the diagram itself ------------------------
'  These are written as a legend rather than as notes attached to a use case.
'  An attached note is pinned next to its use case, and with this many
'  associations that pushed the whole layout out of shape.

legend bottom
  |= |= Assumption recorded on this diagram |
  | 1 | **Signing in is assumed throughout.** Every use case except "Sign in" requires the |
  |   | member of staff to be signed in already. Drawing that as an <<include>> from all |
  |   | seventeen would bury the diagram in arrows, so it is stated once here. |
  | 2 | **Availability is checked twice, deliberately.** The service layer checks first, so |
  |   | the receptionist gets a sentence they can act on. The database checks last, with a |
  |   | UNIQUE index, so two people saving in the same instant still cannot double book. |
  | 3 | **The administrator is a receptionist as well.** The generalisation means every |
  |   | association drawn to Receptionist applies to Administrator too. |
  | 4 | **The email service is a supporting actor.** The system talks to it; it never starts |
  |   | anything. Notifications are switched off until real mail credentials are supplied. |
  | 5 | **Dentists are not actors.** They read the printed day schedule; they do not sign in. |
  |   | Giving them their own login is recorded as future work, not modelled here. |
end legend

@enduml
